INT. THE GILDED MIC KARAOKE BAR - TUESDAY NIGHT

Sticky booths. A disco ball missing half its mirrors. A screen the size of a billboard scrolls lyrics in karaoke-blue. Six strangers share one corner table, an enormous plate of fries between them.

JOSEFINA OLIVEIRA, 30s, a leather jacket and a cautious smile, clutches a song binder. DARREN FITZWILLIAM, 30s, a shirt with too many buttons undone, leans back, king of the booth. MEI-LING CHOW, 20s, cat-eye glasses, sips a mocktail, eyes darting. CHUKWUEMEKA "CHUKS" EZEKWESILI, 30s, in a bright print shirt, drums the table with his thumbs. ANDRE BEAUCHAMP, 40s, quiet, nursing a ginger ale. BETTINA KOWALSKI, 50s, a sequined cardigan, waves her arm at the DJ.

BETTINA
(to the table)
All right, kids. Who's going first?

DARREN
Ladies first.

JOSEFINA
That's sexist.

DARREN
It's chivalrous.

MEI-LING
(muttering)
It's a trap.

CHUKS
I'll go.

ANDRE
Chuks, you've been dying to go since we sat down.

CHUKS
Because I have a gift, Andre. A gift.

BETTINA
Do you have a number?

CHUKS
Eleven. "Dancing Queen."

DARREN
Abba. Brave.

JOSEFINA
He'll destroy it.

CHUKS
I'll honor it.

He bounds to the stage. The screen lights. A familiar piano riff. He grips the mic like a torch. Sings the first line, loud, glorious, a note short of flat. The table roars.

BETTINA ^
Go on, baby!

MEI-LING
(softly)
Oh, he's actually good.

JOSEFINA
He's terrible.

MEI-LING
He's terrible and good.

DARREN
(leaning toward Josefina)
You should do one next.

JOSEFINA
I don't sing.

DARREN
Everyone sings.

JOSEFINA
I sing in the shower.

DARREN
Well, there's no shower here.

JOSEFINA
(dry)
Thank God.

ANDRE
(to Mei-Ling, quietly)
Do you want to go?

MEI-LING
I'd rather die.

ANDRE
I'll go with you.

She stares at him. A small, astonished smile.

MEI-LING
You would?

ANDRE
Duet. Whatever you pick.

MEI-LING
...Something slow.

ANDRE
Slow's good.

BETTINA
(to the whole table)
Look at this! Two matches and a marching band!

DARREN
(under his breath)
Bettina, you're a menace.

BETTINA
That's the nicest thing you've said all night, Darren.

On stage, Chuks hits the chorus. The whole bar sings along, shouting, tipsy, joyous. Josefina, in spite of herself, mouths the words. Darren sees. He grins. She catches him and glares, then laughs.
